.TH ORCH 1 "2026-10-25" "orch" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
orch \- inspect and uninstall command-line tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orch list
[\fB\-\-native\fR | \fB\-\-user\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B orch info
.I name
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-run\fR]
.br
.B orch uninstall
.IR command | package
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR | \fB\-\-yes\fR]
.br
.B orch history
[\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-file\fR \fIpath\fR]
.I command
[\fIargs\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B orch
scans the directories in
.B PATH
and marks every executable it finds as
.I native
(shipped with the operating system) or
.I user
(installed by you). It can show details about a tool and uninstall it through the package manager that owns it.
.PP
.B orch
is not a package manager. It delegates uninstalls to one that is already installed (Homebrew, cargo, npm and pipx) and never deletes files itself.
.PP
Only macOS is supported.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List tools found on
.BR PATH .
When a name appears in several directories, the first one wins. Columns are name, kind, source and path. Source is a guess made from the resolved path: brew, cargo, go, npm, manual or unknown.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-native
Show only tools shipped with the OS.
.TP
.B \-\-user
Show only user-installed tools.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also scan well-known tool directories that are not on
.BR PATH :
~/.cargo/bin, ~/go/bin, ~/.local/bin, ~/.bun/bin, ~/.deno/bin and ~/.dotnet/tools.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of a table.
.RE
.TP
\fBinfo\fR \fIname\fR
Show path, resolved path, kind, source, owning package manager, version and the one-line man page description.
.IP
The version is read by running \fIname\fR \fB\-\-version\fR with a 2 second timeout, which executes the tool. Same-named commands hidden behind the first one on PATH are listed as shadows.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-run
Do not execute the tool to read its version.
.RE
.TP
\fBuninstall\fR \fIcommand\fR|\fIpackage\fR
Uninstall the package that provides a command, using the package manager that owns it. The argument is a command found on
.B PATH
or the name of an installed package, so
.B orch uninstall svt-av1
and
.B orch uninstall SvtAv1EncApp
do the same. Homebrew (formulae and casks), cargo, global npm and pipx are supported. Tools from other sources (go, nvm, asdf, apps) are reported but not removed.
.IP
The whole package is removed, not just the command. Before asking to confirm, orch prints the package, how many commands on PATH belong to it, and the command it will run.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the command and exit without running it.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
\fBhistory\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
Show past calls of a command from shell history, oldest first. With
.I args
only calls whose leading arguments equal them are shown, so
.B orch history git commit
matches calls that start with
.BR "git commit" .
The command is recognized anywhere a command can start: after environment assignments, after
.BR sudo ,
.BR time ,
.B env
and similar wrappers, in pipelines and after
.B &&
or
.BR ; .
History is read from zsh, bash and fish. Options for orch go before the command.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-limit " \fIN\fR"
Show only the last N calls (default 20, 0 for all).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON.
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fIpath\fR
Read this history file instead of the defaults. May be repeated.
.RE
.IP
Shells often write history only when they exit, so the current session may be missing. History can contain secrets typed on the command line.
.SH SAFETY
.B uninstall
refuses native OS tools. If no package manager owns the tool, it prints the path and exits with an error so you can remove the file yourself.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Tool not found, native tool refused, no owner, aborted, or the package manager failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List tools you installed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
orch list \-\-user
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what would be run to remove a Homebrew tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
orch uninstall jq \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /opt/homebrew/Cellar
Used to detect Homebrew ownership from the resolved path.
.TP
.B $HISTFILE, ~/.zsh_history, ~/.bash_history, ~/.local/share/fish/fish_history
Read by
.BR history .
